ursework kind of resembled that  from high school so it wasn't that much  new content to take on and if anything  the ideas were the same it was like  mechanics or electromag a little bit of  modern physics just like I'd done in  high school but just more in-depth one  of the differences going to uni from  high school that was maybe a challenging  thing to come to terms with was sort of  that level of competition that I  personally felt coming into it I'd been  at a small high school and had always  sort of been the top of my class just  because not many people were studying  science or very serious about it then  going into uni they were sort of a lot  of people that were really focused a lot  that word but it was totally a different  environment if you were used to being  the top of your class and then having to  compete with a bunch of people who are  also used to that I definitely have a  lot of stressful moments during the  degree I am the kind of person that does  things at the last minute so for every  test but the whole way through I would  basically cram beforehand and in  hindsight I know that's not a good thing  to do and I miss a lot of things I look  back on I wish I remembered the more and  that I had really taken the time to  learn them and dips but I just didn't I  just couldn't find the time to do that I  just had to sort of memorize chunks of  material that I knew would be relevant  for the exam and then use that I don't  think though doing that was a total  waste though because it enabled me to  get through to get  good grades to keep going and I was  exposed to that material to a level of  understanding that I could go and read  about it again if I wanted to explore  and more in-depth also definitely the  thing that helped me the most studying  was having good friends that are  studying the same thing as you and that  you can actually study in groups with  and bounce ideas off each other all the  courses I hated the most were probably  because I 